<Wiki
  title="puv use"
  description="Install a PHP release and record that exact patch in puv.toml."
>
  <p class="kicker">Wiki</p>
  <h1>use</h1>
  <p>
    <code>puv use</code> selects the project runtime. The argument is a minor line or an exact
    patch. The manifest stores the patch, not the line you typed when you typed a line.
  </p>

  <h2>A minor line</h2>
  <pre class="block">puv use 8.4</pre>
  <p>
    This installs the newest 8.4 patch and records that exact version. After it runs,
    <code>puv.toml</code> contains something like <code>php = "8.4.23"</code>, which is the patch
    that was newest at the time. A later <a href="/wiki/commands/sync"><code>sync</code></a> does
    not float to a newer 8.4 on its own.
  </p>

  <h2>An exact patch</h2>
  <pre class="block">puv use 8.4.23</pre>
  <p>This stays on 8.4.23. It does not move to a later 8.4.</p>

  <h2>Where the runtime comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Runtimes are prebuilt StaticPHP CLI builds for Linux glibc, <code>x86_64</code> and
    <code>aarch64</code>. puv does not compile php-src. The download prints a progress line on
    stderr. A cached archive stays quiet.
  </p>
  <p>
    Installed builds live under <code>~/.local/share/puv/</code>. The project shim is
    <code>.puv/bin/php</code>. Listing every installed runtime, or installing a line without
    changing the project pin, is <a href="/wiki/commands/php"><code>puv php</code></a>.
  </p>
</Wiki>
